  <div class="tx"><h1 class="h">A winter<br>we will never<br>forget.</h1></div>
</header>

<section class="nslider" id="brojke">
  <div class="nstrack" id="nstrack">
    <article class="ns"><b>49</b><div class="nt"><h3>Participating nations</h3><p>49 nations gathered under the Olympic rings, a record at the time for the Winter Games.</p></div></article>
    <article class="ns"><b>1.272</b><div class="nt"><h3>Athletes</h3><p>For twelve days the world’s best winter athletes competed at Olympic venues in Sarajevo and on the surrounding mountains.</p></div></article>
    <article class="ns"><b>39</b><div class="nt"><h3>Olympic events</h3><p>Medals were awarded in 39 events, from alpine skiing on Bjelašnica to figure skating in Zetra.</p></div></article>
    <article class="ns"><b>696</b><div class="nt"><h3>Thousand spectators</h3><p>Almost 700,000 people watched the competitions live on the slopes, ski jumps and in the arenas.</p></div></article>
    <article class="ns"><b>80</b><div class="nt"><h3>Thousand at Malo polje</h3><p>That is how many people came to Igman to watch the Olympic ski jumping live.</p></div></article>
    <article class="ns"><b>10+</b><div class="nt"><h3>Thousand volunteers</h3><p>Without volunteers there would have been no Games. The people of Sarajevo were hosts, guides and the face of the city to the world.</p></div></article>
  </div>
  <div class="nsctl"><span class="nsc" id="nsc">01 / 06</span><i class="nsbar"><b id="nsbar"></b></i><button class="circ l" id="nsp" aria-label="Previous"><svg class="ar" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></button><button class="circ" id="nsn" aria-label="Next"><svg class="ar" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></button></div>
</section>



<section class="story" id="prica">
  <div class="row">
    <div class="arch"><img src="img/publika.jpg" alt="Sarajevo during the Games"></div>
    <div class="rt"><h2 class="h">Sarajevo at the centre of the world.</h2><p class="pp">In 1984 Sarajevo hosted the XIV Olympic Winter Games and became the centre of world winter sport. The city and its surrounding mountains welcomed the best athletes from around the world, and the name of Sarajevo was heard every day far beyond the borders of what was then Yugoslavia. For the city, its people and the whole country it was a moment of exceptional significance, and its legacy is still present today.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="row rev">
    <div class="arch"><img src="img/kosarka-zakucavanje.jpg" alt="Opening ceremony"></div>
    <div class="rt"><h2 class="h">The largest sporting event in the history of Yugoslavia.</h2><p class="pp">Organising the Olympic Winter Games was a sporting and organisational undertaking unlike anything Yugoslavia had experienced before. 1,272 athletes from 49 nations competed, and almost 700,000 spectator visits were recorded at the Olympic venues. For twelve Olympic days Sarajevo lived to the rhythm of competition, bringing local and international audiences together around one shared event.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>



<section class="ob" id="baklja">
  <h2 class="h" data-desk="One flame.<br>One country.">One<br>flame.<br>One<br>country.</h2>
  <div class="stage"><div class="torch3d" id="torch3d" aria-hidden="true"></div></div>
  <p class="cap3">Before arriving in Sarajevo, the Olympic flame travelled through all six republics and both autonomous provinces of what was then Yugoslavia. Its journey connected different cities, regions and generations, bringing the Olympic atmosphere to those who could not attend the Games. The Olympic torch became a symbol of togetherness and of an event that belonged not only to Sarajevo, but to the whole country.</p>
</section>



<section class="band" id="bjelasnica">
  <img src="img/hero-skijanje-desktop.jpg" alt="" style="object-position:62% 40%">
  <div class="bshade"></div>
  <div class="btx"><h2 class="h">Bjelašnica</h2></div>
</section>



<section class="story" style="padding-top:calc(300*var(--u))">
  <div class="row">
    <div class="arch"><img src="img/slalom-staza.jpg" alt="Building the Olympic slopes"></div>
    <div class="rt"><h2 class="h">The mountain that made Olympic history.</h2><p class="pp">Preparations for the Winter Games transformed Bjelašnica. Ski slopes and lifts were built for the Olympic events, and the Famos and Smuk hotels and a press centre for local and international media were built at the foot of the mountain. The mountain gained the infrastructure needed to host the world’s biggest ski races, laying the foundation for the Olympic centre we know today.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="row rev">
    <div class="arch"><img src="img/fis-c.jpg" alt="Alpine skiing"></div>
    <div class="rt"><h2 class="h">The world’s best skiers on our mountain.</h2><p class="pp">During the Sarajevo Olympics, Bjelašnica hosted all the men’s alpine skiing events – downhill, slalom and giant slalom. Its steep slopes brought together the world’s best skiers, while thousands of spectators watched from the surrounding hillsides. The Olympic races introduced Bjelašnica to an international audience and wrote its name into the history of world skiing.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>



<section class="ob" id="jure">
  <h2 class="h" data-desk="Volimo Jureka<br>više od bureka!">Volimo<br>Jureka<br>više od<br>bureka!</h2>
  <div class="stage"><div class="medal3d" id="medal3d" aria-hidden="true"></div></div>
  <p class="cap3"><strong>Silver that was worth gold.</strong>One of the most emotional moments of the Sarajevo Olympics happened on Bjelašnica, when Slovenian skier Jure Franko won silver in the giant slalom. It was Yugoslavia’s first ever Winter Olympic medal and a reason to celebrate across the country. Sarajevans marked his success with a witty slogan – “We love Jure more than burek!” – that outlived the Games and remains a symbol of an unforgettable winter.</p>
</section>

<section class="band" id="igman">
  <img src="img/staza-zastave.jpg" alt="" style="object-position:50% 45%">
  <div class="bshade"></div>
  <div class="btx"><h2 class="h">Igman</h2></div>
</section>

<section class="story" style="padding-top:calc(300*var(--u))">
  <div class="row">
    <div class="arch"><img src="img/staza-zastave.jpg" alt="Olympic ski jumps"></div>
    <div class="rt"><h2 class="h">Two ski jumps. Just 18 months.</h2><p class="pp">Two large ski jumps were built at Malo polje for the Olympic events, designed by Slovenian engineers, the brothers Janez and Vlado Gorišek. This demanding construction project took just a year and a half. Set in a natural mountain amphitheatre, the jumps became the stage for spectacular competitions that drew tens of thousands of spectators.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="row rev">
    <div class="arch"><img src="img/slalom-c.jpg" alt="Nordic events"></div>
    <div class="rt"><h2 class="h">An Olympic story in the forests of Igman.</h2><p class="pp">While Malo polje was the centre of Olympic ski jumping, nearby Veliko polje hosted cross-country skiing, biathlon and Nordic combined. Wide mountain plateaus and dense forests provided a natural setting for these winter sports. Together with Malo polje, Veliko polje made Igman one of the most important mountain venues of the Sarajevo Olympics.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>



<section class="band" id="zetra">
  <img src="img/zetra-klizanje.jpg" alt="" style="object-position:45% 22%">
  <div class="bshade"></div>
  <div class="btx"><h2 class="h">Zetra</h2></div>
</section>



<section class="story" style="padding-top:calc(300*var(--u))">
  <div class="row">
    <div class="arch"><img src="img/zetra-retro.jpg" alt="Building the Zetra hall"></div>
    <div class="rt"><h2 class="h">The most ambitious construction project of the Games.</h2><p class="pp">Built for the XIV Olympic Winter Games, Zetra was the largest single construction project of the Sarajevo Olympics. It was officially opened in 1982, and its distinctive design was the work of architects Lidumil Alikalfić and Dušan Đapa and engineer Osman Morankić. The project received the prestigious Borba award for the best architectural achievement in Yugoslavia, making Zetra a symbol of the building and architecture of its time.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="row rev">
    <div class="arch"><img src="img/kuglana.jpg" alt="Zetra hall"></div>
    <div class="rt"><h2 class="h">More than 145,000 spectators at the Zetra venues.</h2><p class="pp">During the Games, Zetra was the centre of ice sports. The hall hosted figure skating and ice hockey, including the final games of the Olympic hockey tournament, while the world’s best speed skaters raced for medals on the outdoor rink. Over twelve Olympic days the Zetra complex drew more than 145,000 spectators, making it one of the most visited venues of the Sarajevo Games.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>



<section class="ob" id="zatvaranje">
  <h2 class="h" data-desk="„Doviđenja,<br>drago Sarajevo!“">„Doviđenja,<br>drago<br>Sarajevo!“</h2>
  <p class="cap3">After twelve Olympic days, on 19 February 1984, Zetra hosted the closing ceremony of the XIV Olympic Winter Games. Athletes and hosts gathered one last time to mark the end of an event Sarajevo had prepared for over years. IOC President Juan Antonio Samaranch bade farewell to the hosts in the local language – “Goodbye, dear Sarajevo!” – words that remain deeply etched in the city’s collective memory.</p>
</section>

